Chapter 32 – Part 1
Mikado’s building did not improve in the evening.
Same narrow stairwell. Same cookie-size landing. Same door that had been painted once and then given up. Curtis knew the smell of it already — cheap detergent, a kettle, a morning that had ended with Namie Yagiri lighting up his phone and a girl with a pink rose left on the other side of the wood.
He stopped at the foot of the last flight and put a hand out, not quite touching Mikado’s sleeve.
“I’ll wait out here with Iza-chan. And Celty.” The nickname sat in the air like a thumbtack. “If we all go in, we will spook her most likely.”
Mikado looked up the stairs.
The helmet. The fur-trimmed coat. The foreigner who had thrown a ripped jacket in a bin and still walked like the landing belonged to a briefing.
He remembered the handshake that had not been a handshake. You’re safe here. Bad tea. A futon. A girl who had told him she could kick his ass. A wad of cash in a drawer under shirts. A text he had pretended not to see.
If the rider went in first, Mika would see the absence she had been carved to copy.
If Izaya went in first, the room would stop being a door and start being a listing.
“…Yeah.” Mikado nodded once, too fast, then made it a real decision. “Yeah. I’ll go in. I’ll— I’ll tell her it’s me. If she wants you after, I’ll come get you. Just you.”
Izaya’s smile bloomed, wounded on purpose.
“Iza-chan. How familial. And I don’t even get to see the pet.”
“She’s not a pet,” Curtis said, without looking at him. “She’s a person in a one-room. You stay on the landing.”
Celty’s helmet ticked toward the door. The private channel opened, tight.
I want to see her.
I know. That’s why you wait. She saw a table. She heard Mei. She wakes up to your collar and she will think the hunt followed her home.
A pause. Then, typed for the two boys, phone turned out:
We wait.
Mikado swallowed.
“Two minutes,” he said, because that was the number Curtis had used on this landing once already. “If I don’t come back—”
“You come back,” Curtis said. “Or I come in. Those are the only two versions.”
Mikado climbed.
Key. Lock. The little courtesy knock on his own door so he would not walk in like a thief.
The three of them stayed on the cheap concrete with a bicycle wheel and a grimy window.
Izaya rocked on his heels, pleased with the architecture of being excluded.
“You two have a system. How long have you been running other people’s apartments?”
Curtis watched the door.
“Long enough to know you don’t get the first look.”
On a landing that had already held a handoff and a throat-name,
Mikado Ryuugamine went in alone
because the foreigner asked him to be the harmless face
one more time.
Celty Sturluson waited with a head
she now knew had been copied
onto the girl behind the wood.
Izaya Orihara smiled at the closed door
like a man who could afford
two minutes.
And Curtis Nightstar stood in a torn shirt
and called him Iza-chan
so the flea would look at the insult
instead of the room.
The landing held three people and a closed door.
Izaya tipped his head, still smiling at the insult like it was a gift with a ribbon.
“So why do you call me that?”
Curtis smirked.
“Eh. Just for shits and giggles.” A small shrug in the torn shirt. “In a way, the same reason you call Shizuo Shizu-chan. Or whatever.” A beat. Flat. Almost kind. “Except I’m not gay.”
Celty lifted the phone.
Turned the screen so Izaya had to read it.
You’re gay?
Izaya looked at the PDA.
Then at Curtis.
Then at the PDA again, as if the rider had just quoted a sidewalk from weeks ago that she should not have been present for.
“Oh, wonderful. He’s been retelling my life as a punchline and now the Headless Rider is doing follow-up questions.” The smile stayed. The eyes did not enjoy it as much as the mouth. “For the record, fixation is not a dating profile. Shizu-chan is an unfinished question. I have explained this. Some people still prefer the cheap version.”
Curtis leaned a shoulder against the grimy wall.
“You explained it. You still say Shizu-chan like it tastes good.”
Celty typed again.
So not gay.
Just obsessed.
Izaya put a hand over his heart.
“Celty. You wound me. I preferred you when you only kicked people and refused to socialize.” He glanced at the door Mikado had gone through. “And you—” a look at Curtis, brighter now, using the joke to dig “—you pick nicknames the way I pick at monsters. Iza-chan. Shizu-chan. Next you’ll be calling Namie Nami-chan and pretending that’s also shits and giggles.”
Curtis’s smirk thinned by a millimeter.
“Don’t.”
“Ah.” Izaya’s delight came back honest. “A real one. Not gay. Just territorial about other people’s diminutives.”
Celty’s helmet ticked toward Curtis, then back to Izaya. The private line stayed shut. The phone did the work.
Stop talking.
He is still in there.
Izaya hummed.
“The first-year is taking a long two minutes. Either she is hugging him or the apartment is teaching him a lesson.” He rocked on his heels. “Iza-chan can wait. Iza-chan is patient. Iza-chan is not gay. He’s simply collecting the way people flinch when you put -chan on a last name.”
Curtis watched the door.
“Keep collecting out here. You don’t collect what’s behind that wood.”
On a cheap landing, the nickname became a rematch
of a sidewalk trade.
Izaya Orihara denied the word
and kept saying Shizu-chan
anyway.
Celty Sturluson asked it in writing
like a rumor she could pin.
And Curtis Nightstar smirked
like the joke was free —
which it was not.
Not with a flea who filed every
-chan as a tell.
Curtis stared at him.
Izaya smirked back.
The landing held that for a second too long — two people who had already stabbed, traded, refused, and named each other on a sidewalk weeks ago, now stuck outside a first-year’s door with a nickname and a PDA question still hanging in the air.
Then Curtis’s mouth broke.
Izaya’s followed, sharp and helpless in the same breath.
“Fuck you, Izaya. Hahah—”
The laugh came out of him ugly and real, the kind he usually saved for Justice and empty roofs. Izaya laughed too, quieter, delighted that the foreigner had finally spent a sound that wasn’t a rule.
Celty’s helmet turned from one to the other like they had both malfunctioned.
She typed.
What is wrong with you.
Izaya wiped at one eye with a knuckle, still grinning.
“He started it. Iza-chan. Gay. Shizu-chan. It’s a whole syllabus.”
Curtis leaned his head back against the grimy wall, braids shifting, the leftover laugh still in his chest.
“You’re the worst person I’ve met who I don’t actually want dead.” He pointed two fingers at Izaya without heat. “That’s not friendship. That’s a medical condition.”
“I’ll take it,” Izaya said. “Most people skip straight to the dead part.”
The door stayed closed.
The two minutes kept stretching.
On a cheap landing, the board game slipped
and became a laugh neither of them
had budgeted for.
Celty Sturluson watched two men
who should have been enemies
lose the joke
at the same time.
Curtis Nightstar said fuck you
like it was almost
fond.
And Izaya Orihara filed the sound
next to friendship — the currency he still did not carry.
Curtis’s phone buzzed against his thigh.
He almost ignored it. Two minutes. A closed door. Izaya still tasting the leftover laugh.
Then he saw the name.
Mika: curtis?! dont go back to mikados. yagiri. they found me! i got out through a window. two men are waiting for you and mikado.
The landing changed temperature.
“Fuck all kinds of duck.” Curtis’s head snapped up. “Izaya on my left. Celty, get my six.”
Izaya’s smirk arrived on time, bright as a match.
“Oooh? So coordinated.”
Curtis hit the door with his shoulder.
Cheap wood gave like it had been waiting to. The one-room opened on the wrong picture: futon kicked, kettle on its side, Mikado on the floor with a forearm across his throat and a second man already turning toward the sound.
Two of them. Company shoulders. The kind of coats that did not belong in a first-year apartment.
Mikado’s eyes found Curtis and went wide.
“Nigh—!”
Curtis had the first man before the name finished. Fist in the coat, lift, the wall learned a new dent. Breath left the guy in a punched-out sound. Curtis did not look at Izaya. He did not have to.
Izaya took the other like the invitation had been printed on the door.
A twist, a laugh, a heel in the back of a knee. The second man hit the genkan tiles face-first. Izaya’s voice stayed pleasant while he worked.
“Yagiri Pharmaceuticals. You could have knocked. We would have told you the pet already left through the window.”
“She’s not a pet,” Curtis snarled, and drove the first man into the floor hard enough that the cheap table jumped.
Celty filled the doorway behind them, smoke already in her gloves, helmet covering the absence Mikado had already seen. No one came up the stairs. Six held.
The man under Curtis tried for a pocket. Curtis took the wrist and made the pocket a bad idea.
“Mikado.” Flat. Command. “You breathing?”
Mikado coughed, scrambling back until his shoulders hit the desk that was also a table.
“Y-yeah. They— they were already inside. She was gone. They said if I didn’t know where—” His voice cracked. “I didn’t. I don’t.”
The first man spat blood on student flooring.
“Yagiri wants the girl. You picked the wrong apartment, foreigner.”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes did not blink.
“You picked the wrong first-year.”
Izaya pinned his own man with a knee and a smile that had never been about rescue.
“Do we keep them? Question them? Or do we let them run home and tell Namie the door has new furniture?”
Celty’s phone snapped up, screen turned so only Curtis caught the first line in the private channel under it.
Window. She’s gone. These are the same coats.
Out loud, the PDA said:
Don’t kill them here.
This is a student’s room.
Curtis hauled his man up by the collar and looked at the open window — curtain moving, night air, a fire-escape logic Mika would have used without asking permission.
He had told her she could kick a dork’s ass.
He had not told her she would have to vanish twice in one day.
“Celty. Stairs stay yours.” He glanced at Izaya, the laugh from the landing already dead. “Left still means left. If he reaches for a phone, take the hand.”
Izaya’s eyes shone.
“See? Coordinated. I knew you two had a system.”
On a cheap apartment floor that had promised safety at breakfast,
Mika Harima’s warning arrived
one locked door too late.
Mikado Ryuugamine learned what onboard
weighed when the company came in
before the two minutes ended.
Celty Sturluson held the stairs.
Izaya Orihara took a man apart
like a game piece.
And Curtis Nightstar came through the wood because a text had said
don’t go back — and he had gone back anyway.
Curtis’s hand closed on the first man’s neck.
Not a squeeze that ended anything. A question with fingers.
“How did you find this place.”
The man choked on the answer like it might save him.
“S-someone saw them. Out front. This morning. The first-year and the girl. Talking. Before he left for school. We logged the building. That’s all. That’s all—”
Curtis sighed through his teeth.
A landing. A rose. A dork opening a door in indoor clothes. Of course the street had eyes. Yagiri did not need Izaya for a front step.
“Yeah. That’s all.”
He grabbed the second coat with his other hand and started walking.
Shoes scraped student flooring. A shoulder clipped the busted frame. Both men went with him like luggage that had opinions.
Celty’s helmet snapped after him.
The phone came up.
Where are you going?
Curtis did not stop.
“It’s fine. We are just going to talk for a while.” He tipped his chin at the wrecked one-room without turning. “Stay with Mikado. FYI, I’m not going to kill him.”
A glance back. Mismatched eyes found the first-year still on the floor, throat red, kettle on its side.
“Mikado. The floor’s yours.” A dry, almost tired edge. “Now’s your time to shine like you’ve been yearning for.”
Mikado stared at him.
The Dollars sat in his pocket like a live wire. TarouTanaka. The chat. The color he had not confirmed on a stairwell and could not keep as a rumor if a company had already used his front step as a map.
Izaya’s smile went knife-thin in the doorway.
“You’re leaving me the student and taking the coats. How generous. How suspicious.”
“You want the show,” Curtis said, already on the stairs, both men dragging, one of them starting to beg. “It’s in the room. Not in the alley.”
The begging got louder on the first landing down.
“Wait—wait, please, we can call it in, we can say she wasn’t here—”
The other man tried to plant his feet. Curtis did not even shift his grip. The man’s shoes left the concrete for a second and came back convinced.
They had hit him in a one-room and learned nothing.
Now the stairwell taught the rest: they could not overpower him at all. Not together. Not with panic. Not with a company name in their mouths.
Celty stayed in the frame above, smoke tight, phone still in her hand.
Izaya stayed too, because a first-year who had just been told to shine was a jackpot with a computer in the corner.
Curtis hit the bottom door with his shoulder and the night took the three of them — one torn shirt, two coats, and a conversation that would not happen in a student’s kitchen.
On cheap stairs that still smelled like morning tea,
Curtis Nightstar walked a pharmaceutical errand
out of Mikado’s life.
Celty Sturluson held the room.
Izaya Orihara kept the boy.
And Mikado Ryuugamine sat on his own floor with a busted door
and the color in his pocket finally asking to be used.
The alley behind Mikado’s building did not deserve company coats.
Curtis put both men into the concrete hard enough that the wall answered. Brick dust. A trash bag that jumped and settled.
“I should kill both of you.” His voice stayed almost conversational. “Does Namie know I’m with him?”
The one who still had language left shook his head too fast.
“No. No. We— we were surprised to see you here. The job was the first-year and the girl. Not you. Nobody said Nightstar would be on the landing.”
Curtis’s mouth ticked.
“Good. Then killing you both should be easy. And explainable.”
“Wait— wait.” Hands up as far as the grip allowed. “We can give you anything. Money. Names. We can say we never found the building—”
Curtis pulled a folded slip from his pocket and flicked it at the second man’s chest.
“Empty both your accounts. That number. Now.”
They did it because the alternative was the look in his mismatched eyes, and because the stairwell had already taught them they could not make him let go. Phones. Shaking thumbs. The ugly little silence of a transfer going through.
The second man swallowed.
“There. It’s done. See? We’re useful. We’re—”
Curtis took an ankle in his hand and ruined the sentence.
The sound was small and wrong. The man folded around it, noise coming out of him like a cut hose.
“What the fuck— we did what you said!”
Curtis smiled. It was not the laugh from the landing. It was the other one. Cynical. Tired. Mean.
“I never said I wasn’t still going to break you. Or kill you.” He tipped his head. “You’re both trash. You were about to kill my friend even though he begged. Why shouldn’t I finish either of you? Heheh.”
The first man’s voice went high.
“Wait. You’re— you’re sweet on Namie, right?”
Curtis’s smile left.
“You better choose your next words carefully.”
“Okay. Okay.” The man talked like the alley had a timer. “There’s a hit on her. Someone wants her dead. It’s happening tonight. We don’t know who. We don’t know when. Just tonight. That’s all we were told. That’s all—”
Curtis stared at them long enough for the brick to cool.
“Fuck’s sakes.” A breath through his teeth. “Fine. You both live.”
He put the ankle back where it belonged. The man screamed anyway. Curtis did not flinch.
“Stop crying, you big baby. I only dislocated it. It’s back. Tiger balm. Ice. You’re good in a few weeks.” He let go and stepped off them like the conversation had become boring. “Work honest now. You come into my crosshairs again, there is no warning. You yell to your boss about me, you’re done. You talk to info brokers, you’re done. I will find out.”
He lifted his phone, screen turned just enough that they could see he was not bluffing about the transfers.
“You sent me money. That means I have you. Not just the coats. The rest of it. Homes. People you don’t want in this alley. Care to test me?”
He said two addresses. Two last names. Enough of the ordinary attached lives that both men went the color of old paper.
He did not decorate it.
He did not need to.
“Blink wrong and I stop being in a generous mood.” The phone went back in his pocket. “Tonight, Namie lives. You forget this building. You forget the first-year. You forget the girl. You forget my face. Get lost.”
They got lost.
One of them walked like the ground had opinions. Neither of them looked back.
Curtis stood in the alley with a cynical smile that had already died, a bank ping he did not care about, and a last name that had asked him to dinner in a clean car that morning.
A hit.
Tonight.
“Fuck all kinds of duck,” he told the brick.
Then he started walking, already pulling Namie Yagiri’s thread, the two minutes on Mikado’s floor left behind with a busted door and a boy who had been told to shine.
In an alley that would not make the news, Curtis Nightstar bought two lives with pain and a warning.
The coats learned the cost of a first-year’s front step.
And somewhere in Ikebukuro, a woman who had said maybe
had a clock on her that he had not been hired to stop.
The door hung wrong on its hinges.
Mikado sat on his own floor with a red throat and a kettle on its side and listened to two grown men beg their way down his stairs in a foreigner’s grip.
Then the building went quiet in the wrong places.
Celty took one step after the sound.
Izaya’s hand found the edge of her sleeve, light as a suggestion and twice as rude.
“Don’t.” He tipped his chin at the open window instead of the stairwell. “The coats are already a Nightstar problem. If you chase him, you miss the room. If you chase the window, you miss the boy.” His smile was thin. “Yagiri Pharmaceuticals. Same rumor as the van stories. Same company that keeps losing girls and calling it research. Those two will run home and write a report. This—” a glance at Mikado “—this is still live.”
Celty’s helmet turned toward the window. Curtain moving. Night air. A fire escape that had already been used.
The private line reached for a person who was not in the room.
Curtis did not answer it. The alley had him.
She typed for Izaya and Mikado both.
The girl went out the window.
This is Yagiri.
I still need to find her.
Mikado’s hands were shaking. He made them stop by giving them a job.
He crawled to the desk that was also a table. Opened the laptop like it might bite. The screen lit his face in cheap blue.
Izaya watched the first-year the way other people watched fireworks.
Mikado’s mind did the Episode 10 math with extra numbers in it.
The girl with the scar.
Anri’s missing friend.
Curtis saying Yagiri is trafficking humans on the landing.
Curtis saying the Black Rider is involved.
Curtis saying TarouTanaka like a thank-you.
A company that had used his front step as an address.
Chat rumors Kanra had laughed through. Yellow Scarves slashed. People disappearing. A pharmaceutical name that sold medicine on television and bought doors in the dark.
He was not only hiding a classmate.
He had put her in a building the street could see.
Mikado swallowed blood-taste and opened the site he was not supposed to open in front of anyone.
The Dollars homepage looked back at him.
He logged in.
Not as a lurker.
As the person who had built the joke and then watched Ikebukuro take it seriously.
Izaya made a small, delighted sound.
“Oh,” he whispered. “Oh, that’s beautiful.”
Celty’s helmet ticked at the screen, then at Mikado.
Mikado did not look at them. He stared at his own handle like it had finally grown a body.
“…I’m not ruthless,” he said, voice wrecked and trying to be a leader anyway. “Curtis said that. He was right. But I made this. And if Yagiri can walk into my apartment, then a color that was supposed to be a story has to become a net.”
He turned the laptop a fraction, not enough to give Izaya the keys, enough to prove the door he was opening.
“I’m TarouTanaka. I founded the Dollars.” The words came out smaller than the myth and heavier than the one-room. “I can put eyes on a girl with a scar without putting her name in Izaya’s mouth. I can tell people to watch Yagiri vans without telling them why. I can—” his voice cracked “—I can be useful. That was what he meant. Shine.”
Izaya’s grin went incandescent.
“Jackpot,” he said, almost fond. “A first-year from outside the city builds an army of bored phones, hides a reconstructed rumor in a one-room, and logs in while his door is still broken. Nightstar told you to shine and you actually did it. I could cry.”
Celty typed, slower.
I am not here for your color.
I am here for the girl.
She is not my head.
Mikado blinked.
“You— you already know that?”
The helmet did not nod. The phone did the work.
I know enough.
I still need to see her.
Yagiri copied something that belongs to me.
That was as close as she would come to Shinra on a floor with Izaya breathing.
Mikado’s hands hovered over the keys.
“Then help me write it so it isn’t a hunt that gets her killed.” He looked at Izaya, fear and stubbornness sharing the same face. “You don’t get the address she ran to. You don’t get her name if I can help it. You get to watch me use the thing you already wanted to own.”
Izaya laughed under his breath.
“Oh, I don’t need to own it tonight. I only needed to see the face that built it.” He leaned on the broken frame, delighted. “The foreigner takes the coats into an alley. The rider admits the girl isn’t a missing head. The dork opens his secret country. This city is so generous.”
Celty’s smoke licked the windowsill and stopped.
Curtis had said the floor was Mikado’s.
For the next minutes, it was.
On a cheap apartment floor with a door that would not close,
canon found its jackpot and had to share the room
with a rider who already knew the girl was a copy.
Mikado Ryuugamine logged in because Nightstar had told him to shine
and because Yagiri had used his front step as a map.
Izaya Orihara watched a founder stop being a rumor.
Celty Sturluson let the color wake and kept Shinra’s hands off the screen.
Somewhere below them, an alley was still having a conversation.